🧠 What is the Bridge Design Pattern?
Bridge Pattern is a structural pattern that:

Decouples abstraction from its implementation.

Lets both evolve independently.

📦 Real-World Analogy:
A remote control (abstraction) can control different devices (implementations) — TV, Radio, Projector.
The remote doesn’t care how the device works internally.

✅ Key Concepts
Component	Description
Abstraction	High-level interface (e.g., RemoteControl)
Implementor	Low-level interface (e.g., Device)
RefinedAbstraction	Concrete abstraction (e.g., AdvancedRemote)
ConcreteImplementor	Specific implementation (e.g., TV, Radio)

🤔 Bridge vs Adapter Pattern
Feature	Bridge Pattern	Adapter Pattern
Purpose	Separate abstraction from implementation	Make incompatible interfaces compatible
When to Use	Want flexibility to extend abstraction/impl	Already have 2 incompatible interfaces
Design Time / Runtime	Design time	Mostly after implementation
Direction	Build both sides together	Wrap existing object (usually one-sided)

✅ Benefits of Bridge Pattern
Open/Closed Principle: Add new Remotes or Devices without modifying existing code.

Avoids class explosion: Avoids creating subclasses for every combination (e.g., TVRemote, RadioRemote).

Separates abstraction from implementation.